tpma-object-fixed-firmware-reserved: TPMA_OBJECT bit 3 is reserved

Classification: profile setting.

Specification (TPM 2.0 Part 2, Table "Definition of (UINT32) TPMA_OBJECT
Bits"): "3 | Reserved | shall be zero"; Part 2, bit fields: "When the bit
fields do not occupy consecutive locations, a spacer field is defined with a
name of "Reserved." Bits in these spaces are reserved and shall be zero."

Version 185 defines bit 3 of TPMA_OBJECT as fixedFirmware (a draft
attribute of the newer specification; nothing in the version 185 code
evaluates it) and therefore no longer rejects it as a reserved bit. In the
specification version tpm-rs implements (Part 2, TPMA_OBJECT) bit 3 is
reserved, so a public area with it SET is TPM_RC_RESERVED_BITS when it is
unmarshaled (as in ibmswtpm2; the public mstpm20 needs the same patch),
instead of failing
later with another response code (e.g. TPM_RC_SIZE or TPM_RC_ATTRIBUTES).
The bit is put back into TPMA_OBJECT_RESERVED_BITS. Found by differential
fuzzing.
diff --git a/TPMCmd/tpm/include/tpm_public/TpmTypes.h b/TPMCmd/tpm/include/tpm_public/TpmTypes.h
--- a/TPMCmd/tpm/include/tpm_public/TpmTypes.h
+++ b/TPMCmd/tpm/include/tpm_public/TpmTypes.h
@@ -1011,7 +1011,9 @@
 #define TPMA_OBJECT_sign                 ((TPMA_OBJECT)1 << 18)
 #define TPMA_OBJECT_x509sign             ((TPMA_OBJECT)1 << 19)
 #define TPMA_OBJECT_RESERVED_BITS                                                \
-    (~(TPMA_OBJECT_fixedTPM | TPMA_OBJECT_stClear | TPMA_OBJECT_fixedFirmware    \
+    /* tpm-rs differential testing -- profile setting: fixedFirmware (bit 3) */ \
+    /* is reserved, as in the specification version tpm-rs implements.      */ \
+    (~(TPMA_OBJECT_fixedTPM | TPMA_OBJECT_stClear                                \
        | TPMA_OBJECT_fixedParent | TPMA_OBJECT_sensitiveDataOrigin               \
        | TPMA_OBJECT_userWithAuth | TPMA_OBJECT_adminWithPolicy                  \
        | TPMA_OBJECT_firmwareLimited | TPMA_OBJECT_svnLimited | TPMA_OBJECT_noDA \
